[38;5;231m⏺[39m Environment selection: settled — the settings module maps hostname → endpoint.

  Second question. [1mThe fork:[0m how [38;5;153msettings.js[39m actually gets into [38;5;153mapp.js[39m. The repo has no module system on the browser
  side today, so creating a module means picking one, and that choice touches [38;5;153mindex.html[39m and how you open the page
  during development.

  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m uses [38;5;153mexport[39m, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m uses [38;5;153mimport[39m, and [38;5;153mindex.html[39m becomes [38;5;153m<script type="module" [39m
    [38;5;153msrc="app.js">[39m. A genuine module boundary with an explicit dependency, and the natural on-ramp if a bundler ever
    arrives. Cost: module scripts are blocked over [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking stops working —
    you'd need a local server ([38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m or similar). Modules are also deferred and scoped, so [38;5;153mlogin[39m and
    [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m stop being globals, which matters only if something outside the file reaches for them (nothing does
    today).
  - [1mPlain global script[0m — add [38;5;153m<script src="settings.js">[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m; it sets a [38;5;153mwindow.AppSettings[39m object that [38;5;153mapp.js[39m
    reads. Nothing about how you run the page changes, [38;5;153mfile://[39m keeps working. Cost: it's a module by convention only — a
    global with an implicit load-order dependency, and no import statement recording who needs what.

  Both are cheap to reverse; this is a two-file change either way.

  [1mMy recommendation: ES modules.[0m The whole point of the request is a seam you can see and trust, and an [38;5;153mimport[39m makes the
  dependency explicit in a way a [38;5;153mwindow[39m global never does. The [38;5;153mfile://[39m cost is the only real downside, and it's a
  one-command workaround — but it's a change to your workflow, so it's your call rather than mine.
